From f7f15717555749334622f835125185373a8f61e4 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jeffrey Hutchins <complife@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 30 Jul 2026 12:14:01 -0600
Subject: [PATCH 09/12] PRO-5724: fix iOS call-timer locale bug + land ringback
 lifecycle fixes (#7)

* fix(ios): serialize the connect timestamp with a fixed locale (PRO-5724)

getSimplifiedISO8601FormattedTimestamp built its NSDateFormatter with
[NSLocale currentLocale]. Per Apple QA1480, a user locale lets the device's
12/24-Hour Time switch override a fixed format string: on a phone set to
12-hour time "HH" renders 1-12, and because the pattern carries no "a" the
AM/PM designator is dropped outright.

So 14:31 serialized as "02:31:00.000-0600". Call.tsx feeds that straight into
new Date(), cc-mob-app renders Date.now() - initialConnectedTimestamp, and the
in-call timer read 720:03 on a three-second-old call -- exactly 12h 0m 3s.
Reported on PRO-5724 with a screenshot whose own status bar ("2:31", not
"14:31") shows the device was on 12-hour time.

Android never had this: ReactNativeArgumentsSerializer already pins Locale.US.

* fix(ios): confine ringback interruption handling to the main queue

AVAudioSessionInterruptionNotification is not documented to be delivered
on the main queue. ringbackPlayer is a nonatomic property that
stopRingback nils from main-queue delegate callbacks, so reading it from
the notification handler raced that release -- a torn read plus
use-after-free, not the harmless nil no-op a nil-check covers.

Wrap the handler body in dispatch_async to the main queue so every
ringbackPlayer access sits on the same queue as playRingback and
stopRingback. The body re-reads self.ringbackPlayer at execution time
rather than capturing the pointer at scheduling time.

All other ringbackPlayer accesses were verified main-queue-confined:
CXProvider uses setDelegate:self queue:nil and the TVOCall/push-registry
delegates are registered with delegateQueue:nil.

